WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.033
- The metaverse.

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:01.866
- The metaverse.

00:00:01.866 --> 00:00:02.980
- Metaverse.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:03.813
- [Narrator] Everyone in tech

00:00:03.813 --> 00:00:06.020
is talking about these new virtual worlds

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.810
where people will be able
to do plenty of things.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.100
- Work, learn, play, shop.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.640
- [Narrator] But many years before them,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.910
a man had already created an online world

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.910
that was also advertised as
a place to work, play, learn

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:19.743
and shop.

00:00:19.743 --> 00:00:21.550
- Second Life is in
many ways the only kind

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.040
of real metaverse sort of
experience that yet exists,

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.640
at least, especially for grownups.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:27.990
- [Narrator] That's Philip Rosedale,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.380
the founder of Second Life,

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.050
and this is his avatar in the game.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.670
Yes, remember the world you'd enter

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.970
from your Windows XP desktop in 2003?

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:38.740
It had its own virtual economy,

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.380
and some people even said
they made millions of dollars

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.630
trading in it.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.732
Over the years, interest
in the platform waned,

00:00:44.732 --> 00:00:49.290
but Second Life now sees an
opportunity to stage a comeback.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.300
Rosedale is returning to the
world he created 13 years

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.130
after leaving the company
to be a strategic advisor.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.740
His plan is to improve
the platforms experience

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.650
without the use of VR headsets

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.100
and to make it work better on smartphones.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.870
- If you looked aspirationally

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.630
at what people could do with a metaverse,

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.940
Second Life in many ways
is still the one to watch

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.350
and even the one to beat.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.270
- [Narrator] So we look
at Second Life's vision

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.970
and why it's past struggles
can serve as a lesson

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.993
for virtual worlds of the future.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.560
Rosedale began developing
Second Life in 1999,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.170
as he toyed with how to
escape his own reality

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.620
and to see how far he could
push his technical expertise.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.650
This is what an early version
of the game looked like.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:34.330
- I certainly was looking,

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.199
asking myself the question

00:01:36.199 --> 00:01:37.810
of whether I could just
build a whole other world

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.060
and disappear into it.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.550
- [Narrator] Rosedale's goal
was to make this virtual world,

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.130
seen here in 2003,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.830
look as life-like as possible,

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.650
as well as being free from
the limits of reality.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.730
Part of that was giving users the power

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.512
to shape the digital realm.

00:01:51.512 --> 00:01:55.070
- They immediately bought the land

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.280
from underneath all
the things we had built

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.790
and tore them all down to
create their own vision

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.930
of what they wanted the place to be.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:04.450
- [Narrator] The game's virtual economy,

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.520
digital tokens, called Linden dollars,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.650
that can be exchanged for US dollars,

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.410
allows members to earn
while playing the game.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.810
Some forged careers as
virtual real estate barons.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.360
- We're definitely pioneers at Second Life

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.080
in identifying that sort of right

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.306
to take things you'd made with
your hands in a virtual space

00:02:26.306 --> 00:02:30.290
and own them and sell them
to other people freely.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.250
- [Narrator] In 2007, Second
Life had some 1 million

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.993
active monthly users and even
featured in television shows.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.340
- [Male Voice] In my second life,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.920
I was also a paper salesman.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.870
Absolutely everything was
the same, except I could fly.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.840
- [Narrator] But by 2019,
Second Life reported

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.623
it had lost a quarter
of its monthly users.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.440
Reentering the world today
can help us understand why.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.150
It's complicated to use.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.520
Creating an avatar involves using sliders

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.200
that can change shape of
each part of the body.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.970
Trying to change clothes
requires multiple steps

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:06.390
and brings you to a separate marketplace.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.550
It's also glitchy.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.450
In some areas, items take minutes to load,

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.610
and it can be hard to find
people to interact with.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.080
- That the interface is difficult.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.320
- [Narrator] Tech giants like Meta,

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.830
formerly known as Facebook,

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.520
have shared visions of a metaverse

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.380
operating in a completely
new internet environment,

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:27.180
allowing users to move assets
between different networks.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.760
Second Life says this
platform still has potential

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.770
and is betting on an immediate refresh,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.040
starting with trying to
streamline this interface

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.500
and make it more
accessible on smartphones.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.040
It currently doesn't have an
official app in the Apple Store

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.253
or Google Play.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:43.450
Second Life says one of its strengths

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.280
is that avatars offer
more customization options

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:47.560
than other competitors.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.780
For example, the gaming platform, ROBLOX,

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.150
which is also developing
metaverse concepts,

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:54.140
and Horizon by Meta.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.070
For Rosedale,

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.880
continuing to enhance the
already realistic figures

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.210
is key for the platform to stand out.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.810
- I like to focus on this idea

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:03.580
of like, how do I wiggle my hands

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.465
when I talk to you in the metaverse,

00:04:05.465 --> 00:04:08.840
how do we feel visually
connected to each other?

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.500
And I think that work can
be done without VR headsets.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.190
- [Narrator] While Meta
has made VR and AR devices

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.620
an integral part of its
vision for the metaverse,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.510
the creator of Second Life
believes webcams could be used

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.453
to help simulate more
authentic character movements.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:24.520
When it comes to audience,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.170
Second Life is not on the
same scale as its competitors,

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.920
Meta's Facebook, Instagram,
and other services

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.480
have 3.5 billion monthly users combined.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.570
And the two companies don't
have the same resources either.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.730
Second Life has said
it's investing millions

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.210
in improving the platform,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.400
Meta said its initial
spending on the project

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:44.823
is around $10 billion.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:47.950
Second Life has survived over the years

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:50.280
because of a smaller
group of loyal members

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.610
and says it makes money
by taking a commission

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:54.690
on the sales of their creations.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.990
The company said the total
volume of sales and transactions

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:01.848
in its virtual world
was worth $650 million.

00:05:01.848 --> 00:05:04.040
While tech giants say the metaverse

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.350
is set to be a massive global industry,

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:08.870
Rosedale's skeptical that
any company will be able

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.880
to keep large audiences interested

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.630
in these virtual platforms.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.640
- Most of us who have
a comfortable existence

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:19.530
in our real bodies, in the real world,

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.980
are going to still tend to prefer that

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:26.980
virtual worlds are a choice
that you have to make.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:32.490
And it's a very substantial
and considerate choice.

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:34.740
And it's not necessarily something

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.293
that all of us are gonna want to do.

